<span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.6" class="ltx_p"><math id="A7.SS9.pic1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\leftarrow z+\hat{z}\odot\mathcal{P}+{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\gamma}}\cdot(\hat{z}\odot{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">𝐩</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\leftarrow z+\hat{z}\odot\mathcal{P}+{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\gamma}}\cdot(\hat{z}\odot{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">  <span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// base + routed + shared</span> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;"></span></span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A7.SS9.pic1.pic7" class="ltx_picture" color="#000000" height="19.85" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-5.12px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 129.61 19.85" width="129.61"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,19.85) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FF9999;" fill="#FF9999" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.95 L 0 15.9 C 0 18.08 1.77 19.85 3.95 19.85 L 125.66 19.85 C 127.84 19.85 129.61 18.08 129.61 15.9 L 129.61 3.95 C 129.61 1.77 127.84 0 125.66 0 L 3.95 0 C 1.77 0 0 1.77 0 3.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFE6E6;" fill="#FFE6E6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.18 3.95 L 1.18 15.9 C 1.18 17.43 2.42 18.66 3.95 18.66 L 125.66 18.66 C 127.19 18.66 128.43 17.43 128.43 15.9 L 128.43 3.95 C 128.43 2.42 127.19 1.18 125.66 1.18 L 3.95 1.18 C 2.42 1.18 1.18 2.42 1.18 3.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 9.06 5.12)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7.01em;--ltx-fo-height:0.6em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:11.5pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="111.5"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A7.SS9.pic1.pic7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7. Load Balance</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// training only</span><span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.7.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:2.0pt;">
 <math id="A7.SS9.pic1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{i}\leftarrow\frac{1}{BT}{\textstyle\sum_{b,t}}w_{i}(x_{b,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{i}\leftarrow\frac{1}{BT}{\textstyle\sum_{b,t}}w_{i}(x_{b,t})</annotation></semantics></math>  <span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// mean routing prob per expert</span> 
<br class="ltx_break"> <math id="A7.SS9.pic1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{imp}}\leftarrow E\cdot{\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{E}}\bar{p}_{i}^{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">imp</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">E</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">E</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{imp}}\leftarrow E\cdot{\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{E}}\bar{p}_{i}^{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math>  <span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.7.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// importance loss</span> 
<br class="ltx_break"> <math id="A7.SS9.pic1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}\leftarrow{\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{E}}\bar{p}_{i}\log(E\cdot\bar{p}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">KL</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">E</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">E</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}\leftarrow{\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{E}}\bar{p}_{i}\log(E\cdot\bar{p}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>  <span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.7.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// KL-uniform loss</span> 
<br class="ltx_break"> <math id="A7.SS9.pic1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\leftarrow\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}+\alpha\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{imp}}+\beta\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">task</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">imp</mtext></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">KL</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\leftarrow\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}+\alpha\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{imp}}+\beta\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math>  <span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.7.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// total loss</span> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;"></span></span>
<span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Return:<span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="A7.SS9.pic1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A7.SS9.pic1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A7.SS9.pic1.3.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">(loss only during training)</span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span id="A7.SS9.fig1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LiME forward pass and training.</span> Colors indicate: <span id="A7.SS9.fig1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#E3ECF4;">frozen</span>, <span id="A7.SS9.fig1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#FBECDE;">trainable</span>, <span id="A7.SS9.fig1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#DFF3E7;">routing (0 params)</span>, <span id="A7.SS9.fig1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFE6E6;">training only</span>. Load balancing (Step 7) prevents expert collapse.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Algorithm Details</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix provides a detailed description of the LiME forward pass and training procedure presented in Algorithm 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">LiME transforms any PEFT-adapted linear layer into a mixture-of-experts layer with <span id="A8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">zero additional routing parameters</span>. The key insight is that routing signals can be derived directly from existing computations—the frozen base output <math id="A8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> and the PEFT output <math id="A8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}</annotation></semantics></math>—eliminating the need for a learned router network.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The algorithm takes a batch of token sequences <math id="A8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times d_{\text{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times d_{\text{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> as input, where <math id="A8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the batch size, <math id="A8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the sequence length, and <math id="A8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input dimension. The pretrained linear layer <math id="A8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.25,1}\mathbf{W}}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{o}}\times d_{\text{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E40FF;" mathcolor="#1E40FF">𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.25,1}\mathbf{W}}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{o}}\times d_{\text{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> remains fixed throughout training, preserving knowledge learned during pretraining while allowing task-specific adaptation through PEFT. The trainable components include: (1) PEFT parameters <math id="A8.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\phi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\phi}}</annotation></semantics></math> from any parameter-efficient method (LoRA, Adapter, Prefix Tuning, IA³, etc.); (2) expert modulators <math id="A8.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> where each <math id="A8.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{o}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{o}}}</annotation></semantics></math> modulates the PEFT adaptation for expert <math id="A8.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>; (3) an optional shared modulator <math id="A8.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{o}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">𝐩</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{o}}}</annotation></semantics></math> providing baseline adaptation; and (4) a learnable scalar <math id="A8.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\gamma}}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\gamma}}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> controlling the shared modulator contribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1</span> computes the frozen linear transformation <math id="A8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z={\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.25,1}\mathbf{W}}_{0}x\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times d_{\text{o}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E40FF;" mathcolor="#1E40FF">𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z={\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.25,1}\mathbf{W}}_{0}x\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times d_{\text{o}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This computation is shared with standard inference and adds no overhead. The output <math id="A8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> serves dual purposes: the base representation for the final output and a routing signal encoding pretrained knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2</span> applies the PEFT module to produce task-specific adaptations <math id="A8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}={{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\delta}}}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times d_{\text{o}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}={{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\delta}}}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times d_{\text{o}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For LoRA, this is <math id="A8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}=(x{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{A}}^{\top}){\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{B}}^{\top}\cdot(\alpha_{\text{LoRA}}/r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">𝐁</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>LoRA</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}=(x{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{A}}^{\top}){\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{B}}^{\top}\cdot(\alpha_{\text{LoRA}}/r)</annotation></semantics></math>. The PEFT adaptation captures task-relevant features and also serves as a routing signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3</span> computes routing weights from the first <math id="A8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions of <math id="A8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, requiring no additional parameters. We first normalize by the infinity norm to ensure stable routing: <math id="A8.p6.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{z}_{1:E}=z_{[:,:,1:E]}/\|z_{[:,:,1:E]}\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{z}_{1:E}=z_{[:,:,1:E]}/\|z_{[:,:,1:E]}\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.p6.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{\hat{z}}_{1:E}=\hat{z}_{[:,:,1:E]}/\|\hat{z}_{[:,:,1:E}]\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\hat{z}}_{1:E}=\hat{z}_{[:,:,1:E]}/\|\hat{z}_{[:,:,1:E}]\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. The normalized signals are combined and passed through softmax: <math id="A8.p6.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w=\mathrm{softmax}(((1-\gamma_{r})\cdot\tilde{z}_{1:E}+\gamma_{r}\cdot\tilde{\hat{z}}_{1:E})/\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\mathrm{softmax}(((1-\gamma_{r})\cdot\tilde{z}_{1:E}+\gamma_{r}\cdot\tilde{\hat{z}}_{1:E})/\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. The routing balance <math id="A8.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the information source: <math id="A8.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{r}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{r}=0</annotation></semantics></math> routes based on pretrained features only, <math id="A8.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{r}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{r}=1</annotation></semantics></math> uses PEFT features only, and <math id="A8.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{r}\in[0.6,0.8]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{r}\in[0.6,0.8]</annotation></semantics></math> provides optimal performance by combining both signals (§<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Ablation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Lightweight Mixture of Experts for Efficient Multimodal Multi-task Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). Our ablation study (§<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Ablation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Lightweight Mixture of Experts for Efficient Multimodal Multi-task Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) demonstrates that routing features can be drawn from any subset of the feature space—leading, central, trailing, or random dimensions all achieve comparable performance—supporting our low-rank redundancy hypothesis.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 4</span> performs Auto Top-K selection, adaptively selecting experts based on relative routing weights: <math id="A8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\theta}=\{i:w_{i}\geq\theta\cdot\max_{j}w_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\theta}=\{i:w_{i}\geq\theta\cdot\max_{j}w_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This selects all experts whose routing weight is at least <math id="A8.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> times the maximum weight. The selected weights are renormalized: <math id="A8.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}_{i}=w_{i}/\sum_{j\in\mathcal{S}_{\theta}}w_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}_{i}=w_{i}/\sum_{j\in\mathcal{S}_{\theta}}w_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. When routing is confident, fewer experts are activated; when uncertain, more experts contribute.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 5</span> combines expert modulators using renormalized routing weights: <math id="A8.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}_{\theta}}\tilde{w}_{i}\cdot{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times d_{\text{o}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}_{\theta}}\tilde{w}_{i}\cdot{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times d_{\text{o}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 6</span> produces the final output by combining the base representation with modulated PEFT adaptation: <math id="A8.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=z+\hat{z}\odot\mathcal{P}+{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\gamma}}\cdot(\hat{z}\odot{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">𝐩</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=z+\hat{z}\odot\mathcal{P}+{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\gamma}}\cdot(\hat{z}\odot{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{p}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A8.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> denotes element-wise multiplication. The three terms represent frozen base output, expert-modulated adaptation, and shared adaptation respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 7</span> applies load balancing during training only. We compute mean routing probabilities <math id="A8.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{i}=\frac{1}{BT}\sum_{b,t}w_{i}(x_{b,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{i}=\frac{1}{BT}\sum_{b,t}w_{i}(x_{b,t})</annotation></semantics></math>, then apply importance loss <math id="A8.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{imp}}=E\cdot\sum_{i=1}^{E}\bar{p}_{i}^{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>imp</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{imp}}=E\cdot\sum_{i=1}^{E}\bar{p}_{i}^{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math> (zero when uniform) and KL-uniform loss <math id="A8.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}=\sum_{i=1}^{E}\bar{p}_{i}\log(E\cdot\bar{p}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}=\sum_{i=1}^{E}\bar{p}_{i}\log(E\cdot\bar{p}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. The total loss is <math id="A8.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}+\alpha\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{imp}}+\beta\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>imp</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}+\alpha\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{imp}}+\beta\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Ablation studies (§<a href="#S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" title="Load Balancing Coefficient (Figure b-c). ‣ 4.1 Ablation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Lightweight Mixture of Experts for Efficient Multimodal Multi-task Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) show optimal coefficients around <math id="A8.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta\approx 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta\approx 0.01</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A8.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complexity.</span> For a single LiME layer with PEFT parameters <math id="A8.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\phi|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\phi|</annotation></semantics></math>, the parameter count is:</p>
<table id="A8.Ex88" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A8.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\phi}}_{\text{LiME}}|=\underbrace{|\phi|}_{\text{shared PEFT}}+\underbrace{E\cdot d_{\text{o}}}_{\text{expert modulators}}+\underbrace{d_{\text{o}}+1}_{\text{shared modulator }+\gamma}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9C27FF;" mathcolor="#9C27FF">ϕ</mi><mtext>LiME</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>shared PEFT</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>expert modulators</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mtext>shared modulator </mtext><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\color[rgb]{0.6133,0.1523,1}\mathbf{\phi}}_{\text{LiME}}|=\underbrace{|\phi|}_{\text{shared PEFT}}+\underbrace{E\cdot d_{\text{o}}}_{\text{expert modulators}}+\underbrace{d_{\text{o}}+1}_{\text{shared modulator }+\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.p11.2" class="ltx_p">In contrast, traditional MoE-PEFT requires <math id="A8.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\cdot|\phi|+\underbrace{d\cdot E}_{\text{learned router}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>learned router</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\cdot|\phi|+\underbrace{d\cdot E}_{\text{learned router}}</annotation></semantics></math> parameters. Routing cost is <math id="A8.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T\cdot E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T\cdot E)</annotation></semantics></math> for token-level, <math id="A8.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T/n\cdot E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T/n\cdot E)</annotation></semantics></math> for n-gram, and <math id="A8.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(E)</annotation></semantics></math> for sequence-level routing. Since <math id="A8.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\ll d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\ll d</annotation></semantics></math> typically, routing overhead is negligible. During inference, Step 7 is skipped entirely, with no additional overhead compared to standard PEFT except for lightweight expert modulation.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A9" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix I </span>Extended Related Work</h2>

<div id="A9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning.</span> PEFT methods adapt large pre-trained models by updating only a small subset of parameters. LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> introduces low-rank decomposition for weight updates, while adapters insert lightweight modules between layers. Subsequent works extend these ideas: VeRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kopiczko et al., 2023</a>)</cite> uses learnable scaling vectors, LoRA-FA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> freezes the down-projection matrix for efficiency, DoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> decomposes weights into magnitude and direction, and Tied-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Renduchintala et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduces weight tying. Non-adapter-based methods like BitFit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken et al., 2022</a>)</cite> tune only bias terms. Recent insights emphasize minimal perturbations, with diagonal rescaling matching complex adapters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kowsher et al., 2025d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kowsher et al., 2025e</a>)</cite> and representation fine-tuning enabling targeted interventions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A9.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mixture of Experts.</span> MoE architectures employ multiple specialized sub-networks with routing mechanisms that selectively activate relevant experts for each input. Foundational MoE concepts date to early supervised learning with specialized networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacobs et al., 1991</a>)</cite>, evolving into modern sparse activations in transformers like Mixtral 8x7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, ST-MoE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zoph, 2022</a>)</cite>, and m-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, enabling scalable width without proportional computational increase.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A9.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MoE-PEFT.</span> Recent work combines MoE with PEFT to achieve input-specific adaptation. MoELoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2024</a>)</cite> uses contrastive objectives to prevent expert collapse in reasoning tasks. MoLE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> employs hierarchical gating for compositionality. MixLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> inserts top-k routed experts per block with load-balancing losses. LoRAMoE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> mitigates knowledge forgetting via plugin experts. MoLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduces layer-wise expert allocation. MoRAL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> addresses lifelong learning scenarios. TT-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kunwar et al., 2025</a>)</cite> decouples training for continual learning. PESC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> enables sparse model transitions in instruction tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A9.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations of Existing MoE-PEFT.</span> Existing methods share three limitations: (i) <span id="A9.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">parameter explosion</span>, with expert parameters scaling linearly with expert count (<math id="A9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times|\phi|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times|\phi|</annotation></semantics></math>); (ii) <span id="A9.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">router overhead</span> from learned routers adding parameters and auxiliary losses to prevent collapse; and (iii) <span id="A9.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">architecture dependence</span>, restricting applicability to adapter-based methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A9.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LiME.</span> We address these limitations by modulating a shared PEFT output with lightweight expert vectors (reducing expert parameters to <math id="A9.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times d</annotation></semantics></math>), deriving zero-parameter routing directly from frozen and adapted representations, and supporting any PEFT method including non-adapter approaches like BitFit. Unlike prior work using learned routers, LiME eliminates routing parameters entirely while incorporating load balancing losses, Auto Top-K for adaptive expert selection based on confidence, and n-gram windowed routing for regularization.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A9.T20" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A9.T20.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 20</span>: </span><span id="A9.T20.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Hyperparameter configuration for LiME experiments.</span></figcaption>
